  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 143 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAs the long-term period begins, our area remains situated between \nthe eastern periphery of a retreating mid-to-upper level ridge over \nsouthern Texas and a strengthening coastal low shifting \nnortheastward. At the surface, the wedging pattern over our area \nwill begin to break down on Friday as northeast winds become more \nvariable under relative high pressure. The sensible weather impacts \nof this pattern will be continued drier and cooler conditions under \nstrong subsidence through the weekend as a Canadian airmass is cut \noff and shifted toward our area along the backside of the low. As a \nresult, expect more seasonable early fall temperatures, with highs \nranging from the low 70s over our mountain counties to the low-to-\nmid 80s across the rest of the CWA through the weekend. Morning lows \nwill dip into at least the mid-to-upper 50s over the bulk of our \narea through early next week. As an aside, the last time the Atlanta \nMetro dropped into the 50s for morning lows was June 3rd, so this \ncooler air is a welcomed addition to the long-term forecast. \n\nWith drier conditions in place, precipitation chances remain limited \nthrough the middle of next week, capped at around 10% for isolated \nconvection areawide. By late next week, PWAT values and rain chances \nwill be on the increase as surface winds shift to the south ahead of \na potential approaching cold front.",
